[#:seon.task{:name "a-jvm-run-answers-evaluated-but-not-recorded"
             :kind :function
             :intent "Two MCP JVM-mode evaluations (lane health-noise, 2026-10-06) answered 'evaluated but not recorded' (`:seon.batch/applied` true, :tx-count 0) after a 2.6 s form returning a large map: a run answered without its one synchronous settlement, which CLAUDE.md requires to be durable before the run answers. Reproduce with a JVM-mode form that runs ~2-3 s and returns a large map; find which stage drops the settlement (size bound? timeout?) and fix at that producer so the run settles or answers a declared error. Done when the repro settles (the evaluation is stored) or returns the named error alternative, with a regression."
             :must-read #{seon.batch/evaluate-batch seon.turn.batch/settle}}
 #:seon.task{:name
            "an-earlier-redefinition-of-a-host-function-fails-as-a-core-fault",
            :kind :function,
            :intent
            "produced-failures records a form's failure as a core fault when its producer is a compiled host Var this batch has not redefined. An agent that redefined a host function (e.g. `my.fs/read`) in an EARLIER batch and whose interpreted version then fails is still recorded as a core fault, and root is notified, although the failing code is the agent's. Correct: compare the definition digest that ran (interpreted on the branch) with the compiled Var's; a branch-only version is the agent's own mistake, delivered only. Proof: defn a host function's replacement in one evaluation, call it failing in the next, assert no signature is recorded.",
            :must-read #{seon.batch/produced-failures}}
 #:seon.task{:name "a-deftest-fixture-observation-is-dropped-by-the-runtime-batch"
  :kind :function
  :intent "`seon.fn-test/fixture-observations-survive-static-admission-and-the-fold` (fn_test.clj:1656) is red on HEAD and on default: the runtime batch entity for a deftest carrying ^{`:seon.test/fixture-observation` ...} has no `:seon.test/fixture-observation` (line 1671 asserts it). Found while fixing the check JVM's dependency analysis (410 pass / 2 fail / 8 error, then 494 / 1 / 0). Correct: the metadata reaches the stored test entity. Done when that deftest is green."
  :must-read #{seon.batch/evaluate-batch}
  :evidence #{"docs/research/agent-platform/2026-10-07/check-analysis/analysis-notes.md"}}
 #:seon.task{:name "a-request-cost-counts-only-the-evaluation-arm"
             :kind :function
             :intent "`seon.batch/arm-measures` sums the allocation of the evaluation arms only, so `:seon.cost/alloc-mb` of a request that defines one function and one test reads 22 MB while the JVM allocated 1.0 GB (default f54e73c83, 2026-10-09: 709 MB on virtual threads in the test step, 296 MB on pool threads, 37 MB on the writer thread; JFR in the evidence). The agent cannot see the cost its request caused. Done when `seon.batch/request-cost` carries the allocation of the stages that ran for the request (test step and settlement included) or names the stages it excludes as data, and the 1.0 GB request reports within 2x of it."
             :must-read #{seon.batch/arm-measures seon.batch/request-cost seon.test/run}
             :evidence #{"docs/research/agent-platform/2026-10-09/slow-alloc.md"}}

 #:seon.task{:name "an-unchanged-interpreted-definition-shows-no-cost-entry"
             :kind :function
             :intent "`seon.batch/cost-diff` flags every sample of an interpreted scope (`:seon.profile/context`) as :interpreted and emits it, so a definition the agent did not change comes back as an entry whenever it ran interpreted, against the \"unchanged is unchanged\" and \"false regressions are the bug\" rulings (plan §7). Reproduced on default 6aca2052 as agent chain5-findings: after defining nothing but one scratch var, `my.program/search` and `my.program/match-row` came back as `:seon.cost/diff` entries though main stores `[my.program/search :seon.profile/context]` under the same digest, and root's `prepare-merge!` and `accept-merge!` replies listed `my.program/search` and five `seon.test-support` helpers though only `my.program/match-row` changed. Cause: `cost-diff` decides :changed by comparing a sample digest with main's SAME-scope sample digest; an interpreted scope has no main value (main's functions are compiled), so the missing sample reads as :new and :interpreted alone admits the entry. Correct, in `seon.batch/cost-diff`: it takes an optional fourth argument `main-digests` (`[:map-of :qualified-symbol :seon.program/definition-digest]`, absent means {}); the digest main holds for a symbol is the digest of its stored sample (warm, then cold; scope :seon.profile/context, then :seon.profile/host), else `main-digests`' entry; a live sample's flags are :changed when main holds a digest that differs, :new when main holds none, plus :interpreted for the interpreted scope; an entry is emitted only for :changed, or for :new in the interpreted scope (a compiled :new sample stays left out). The callers `seon.mcp/with-diff` and `seon.context/branch-cost` pass main's program row digests (`seon.db/definition-digest-of`, read once beside `seon.db/stored-costs`) for the live symbols. Existing rows that asserted the old behavior are updated in this task's proofs (they are the next agent's to keep green): `seon.request-cost-test/after-a-restart-the-first-call-compares-against-the-stored-cold-value` now changes the digest (a definition the agent changed, called once, compares its cold sample with main's cold sample: :cold-self-us ratio 1.04, flags #{:changed :interpreted}); `seon.request-cost-test/an-interpreted-live-value-is-never-given-a-ratio-against-a-compiled-main-value` first row now expects flags #{:changed :interpreted} (main defines the symbol, so it is not :new; still no :main and no :ratio across scopes). Proofs: `seon.request-cost-test/an-interpreted-sample-of-the-digest-main-holds-shows-no-entry`, `seon.request-cost-test/a-sample-is-judged-against-the-digest-of-main-s-program-row`, and the two updated rows above (red on HEAD 450dca226, green with the body described, checked by redefining it at an agent's REPL). Not this task: `seon.request-cost-test/an-entrances-self-time-is-never-given-a-ratio` is red on main before and after (the ratio 4.12 for `seon.turn.batch/jvm-evaluate`); it belongs to `seon.batch/an-entrances-self-time-is-its-forms-work`, which edits the same function, and the merge gate will report it red, so land that task first or merge them together."
             :must-read #{seon.batch/cost-diff seon.mcp/with-diff seon.context/branch-cost seon.db/stored-costs seon.db/definition-digest-of}}
 #:seon.task{:name "an-entrances-self-time-is-its-forms-work"
             :kind :function
             :intent "An entrance whose self time is the evaluated form's own work must never be given a ratio. Root's JVM-mode reply that ran `seon.competency/start-run!` (chain run 5, 2026-10-10) carried `seon.turn.batch/jvm-evaluate` :cold-self-us 106063.0 against main's 25739.0, ratio 4.12, #{:changed}; the self time of `seon.turn.batch/jvm-evaluate` is whatever form it evaluates (two different forms are not like for like), so the ratio is a false regression (plan §7: only reliable, like-for-like data is surfaced). Main now stores 106063.0 for it, so the next JVM form compares with that form's work. Rule to name in the owner: a function declared an entrance (its job is to run a caller's form: `seon.turn.batch/jvm-evaluate` and the SCI evaluation entrances) is declared once as data at its definition (`:seon.profile/entrance? true` in its schema attribute map, read where `seon.profile/cell` is made), `seon.batch/cost-diff` gives its samples no :ratio and the explicit `:seon.cost.comparison/unknown :entrance`, and the entrance's self time is not what a reply's cost shows as the form's cost (that is the reply's own `:seon.cost/entrance`). Decide in the owner whether the entrance sample is stored at all."
             :must-read #{seon.batch/cost-diff seon.profile/cell seon.profile/transaction-observation seon.turn.batch/jvm-evaluate}
             :evidence #{"docs/seon/plan.md"}}
 #:seon.task{:name "every-world-is-built-by-one-constructor"
             :kind :function
             :intent "Card W1 (runtime-follows-db §3b, §10; root option A, 2026-10-11, after 4347f6cce). One world constructor `seon.turn.world/world` returns the closed base world (registered schema :seon.batch/world-base): the `seon.batch/world-members` it is given plus the identities and custody the stages read, never a database value (`:seon.db/db` leaves `seon.batch/world-members`; the database is the connection's value read at the call by `seon.call-preparation/supply`). Stages keep adding their keys (ordinal, form sink, reads, scope) to the open :seon.batch/world. It replaces `seon.turn.world/agent-world`, `seon.turn.world/jvm-world` (caller `seon.mcp`), `seon.turn.world/test-agent-world`, `seon.turn.world/merge-world`, the inline world map in `seon.turn/evaluate-sources` and `seon.flow/proc-world`; `seon.test/bounded-result` binds its member a full world from it for the member's branch, deleting its sink-only world. `seon.cluster.agent/acquire-context!`, `seon.cluster.host/host!` and `seon.cluster.boot/request-world` build executions and environments, not worlds, and stay. Proofs: this census test (every binding of `seon.batch/*world*` in the loaded program is the constructor's value or a stage's assoc onto a bound world) and a new row in seon.test-test/a-member-runs-in-its-own-world-never-its-requesters asserting the member's world is the constructor's value for its branch. World construction cost measured before and after. Files: src/seon/turn/world.clj, src/seon/turn.clj (evaluate-sources), src/seon/flow.clj, src/seon/mcp.clj, src/seon/test.clj (bounded-result), resources/seon/schemas/seon.batch.edn, test/seon/batch_test.clj, test/seon/test_test.clj."
             :must-read #{seon.batch/world-members seon.call-preparation/supply seon.turn.world/agent-world seon.turn.world/jvm-world seon.turn/evaluate-sources seon.test/bounded-result}
             :evidence #{"docs/research/agent-platform/2026-10-11/runtime-follows-db.md"}}]
